  "slug": "docking-dynamics-049f",
  "summary": "Three items extend cancer mutation modeling: joint SBS/DBS/ID inference, sequence-based substitution prediction, and a harmonized canine exome atlas.",
  "synthesis": "The items are heterogeneous, but the dominant thread is modeling somatic mutation from genomic data. MOTET is a Bayesian framework that jointly infers multimodal processes linking SBS, DBS and ID signatures across 2,583 PCAWG whole genomes. EAGLE-MUT trains DNA language models on sparse somatic substitution data from esophageal adenocarcinoma and melanoma to predict sequence-context-dependent mutation probabilities. The Canine Cancer Genome Atlas is a distinct, resource-oriented item: harmonized whole-exome data from 667 tumor-normal pairs across 10 histotypes, with 39 drivers reported. What is new is the move from single-class signature decomposition toward joint multi-class inference and sequence-derived mutation probabilities, alongside a cross-species cohort. The summaries do not show how the canine resource relates to these methods, and evidence is mostly preprint-level.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-17"
}
